n to d227effd000e8288ee0e8c4bec99f86e1cc05e9e when a sibling landed 3343b58 mid-tick. Content is identical — git diff dc9e0ea d227effd000e8288ee0e8c4bec99f86e1cc05e9e -- patches/ is empty and the committed patch still sha256 f7f1dfc3.... The Tier2 verdict 18ef167d remains bound to the pre-rebase SHA dc9e0ea, which still resolves as an object and carries the same two files.","qa_note":"Reopened 2026-11-05 by QA tick code-factory-duckbrain-qa-2026-11-05-11-35-44: AC6's second conjunct does not hold at HEAD 81dd425e or at either of this row's own commits (d227effd, dc9e0ea) — `git apply --check patches/fleet-ops-shell-toolkit.patch` exits 1 with `error: fleet_ops_check.py: No such file or directory`, because no commit contains the path the patch targets (`git ls-files | grep -c fleet_ops_check` -> 0; `git cat-file -e <sha>:fleet_ops_check.py` -> absent at all three). AC0/AC1/AC2/AC5 re-verified green at 81dd425e (kind=cli, kind_basis=shell-toolkit:15, fleet cli=7 docs=40). The patch content itself is honest (reverse-apply roundtrip reproduces the pristine digest and restores the live file byte-for-byte). Repair is tracked by CF-241."}
 {"id":"CF-207","title":"Scope ruling: the restricted inference proxy is OUT of the node test path — testing proves transport and execution, not inference","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Owner ruling 2026-11-04: 'i dont think this is in the critical path for testing we will at some point setup a proxy for those instances but for the moment we are fine with out that'. Measured context: cf-inference-proxy.service is active on the control host, but no artifact in the tree proves the node->proxy->gateway->provider edge end-to-end, and CF-171 records that the allowlist/revocation claim has none either. The quorum round (5 seats, 5 families) listed it as a missing dependency; the owner has now ruled it deferred rather than blocking.","acceptance_criteria":"1. A node test exists and passes on cf-node that proves directive receipt and command execution with no inference proxy in the path; its transcript names the commands run and the exit codes.\n2. The dependency map (code-factory-dependency-map-2026-11-04-v4.html) and the verification handbook render the proxy as DEFERRED with this ruling's date, not as a blocker.\n3. No open row on the code-factory, scheduler, task-router or crier boards names the inference proxy as a blocker for node testing (grep -i 'inference proxy' over those boards returns only this row and CF-002/CF-171).\n4. CF-002 and CF-171 remain open and are not closed by this ruling.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-05T00:24:19Z","updated_at":"2026-11-05T00:24:19Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"6b44d93ba84a2418bad9b036d5193da512532ad1a37ca4710cf06ec83fe5bf48"},"capability_tags":[],"commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null}
 {"id":"CF-208","title":"DuckBrain access model for nodes: central over Tailscale with a host roster, or a per-project instance inside the bunker — and the namespace must be scoped, which it is not today","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Owner directive 2026-11-04: nodes should be able to reach DuckBrain over Tailscale on a main or chosen host that runs it for everyone, and the main host keeps 'a list of stuff'; alternatively a node can run DuckBrain locally inside the bunker for its own project, holding only the namespace that project needs and not the others. Measured tonight: duckbrain-http listens on 0.0.0.0:3000 (a tailnet GET to 100.97.236.14:3000 returned 200) with --auth=apikey, and a socat bridge also exposes 100.97.236.14:3001. BUT tokens are not namespace-scoped: the 9router-foreman token returned 200 for namespace=code-factory AND 200 for namespace=tatata. No allowedNamespaces/scoping feature was found under ~/duckbrain/src. So 'the correct namespace and not the others' is convention today, not a control.","acceptance_criteria":"1. A roster exists (JSONL or TOML) listing every project: DuckBrain mode (central|local), endpoint, namespace, and the env/file the token comes from. Resolving code-factory from the roster is one command, and /project/code-factory is present.\n2. Central mode is proven from a NODE: bunker exec on cf-node curls the chosen host over Tailscale with the project token, gets 200 on /project/code-factory, and is REFUSED (403/404) on another project's namespace. Both attempts are in the evidence.\n3. Local mode is proven: a DuckBrain instance inside the bunker starts with exactly one project namespace present, and its data directory holds no other project's namespace.\n4. Tick receipts still land in whichever mode the lane uses: a scheduler tick receipt key is written and read back by key after the change.\n5. The dependency map and the verification handbook name the chosen mode for code-factory and the date of the ruling.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-05T00:26:01Z","updated_at":"2026-11-05T00:26:01Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"6e42c9a18d1e019f6b9f9e1d8eee2221a3223540468ffcefa6a8eeb8e8b3a904"},"capability_tags":[],"commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null}
 {"id":"CF-209","title":"The repo is a TEMPLATE for any coding-hermes operator, not our build log — README, board and quick start must work for a stranger with their own fleet","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Owner directive 2026-11-04: 'this repo is not just for us it is for anyone running coding hermes that wants a full code factory fleet going so this repo is all the tools and stuff it is not just for storing the progress of where our projects are it is just the base template for people what want the same feel of managing a fleet of coding agents'. Measured: README opens correctly ('the factory itself — the specs that define a node, the board that tracks the build, and the scripts that make a node real') but the second clause ties the shipped board to OUR build; the board carries 183 rows of our lane history; 16 rows embed absolute /home/kara paths (CF-086) and 27 tracked files carry internal worktree names (CF-050); the master lineage leak (CF-163) means every clone also gets 772 commits of our internals. A stranger cloning this today inherits our backlog and our hostnames.","acceptance_criteria":"1. README's first screen states what the repo is for an outside operator and what they must supply (control host, CRIER_URL, bunker server, namespace), with no reference to our lanes.\n2. The shipped board is empty of our rows, or every row is explicitly labelled an example, so a lane cannot mistake our history for the operator's work.\n3. The quick start runs from a fresh clone on a host that is not ours, against the operator's own CRIER_URL and BUNKER_SERVER, and never reads our namespace or our board path.\n4. No tracked doc carries our absolute host paths or internal worktree names (binds CF-050 and CF-086).\n5. README lists the components with a one-line contract each and a link into specs/ for the full form.\n6. A clone carries only the factory's own history — the unrelated master lineage is gone (binds CF-163).","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-05T00:27:22Z","updated_at":"2026-11-05T00:27:22Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"754c31567996c3109224ff4f15293bbe4116642d89aae423f2da4be7e4e52286"},"capability_tags":[],"commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null}
